---
/**
 * /benchmarks/ — the evidence behind EDDI's performance and cost claims.
 *
 * English only (localized={false}).
 *
 * Deliberate editorial position: this page publishes the literature and the
 * measurement plan, and states plainly that EDDI's own numbers are not yet
 * measured. It cites results that cut against EDDI's claims as well as for
 * them. A benchmarks page that only flatters the vendor is the kind a
 * technical buyer discounts on sight.
 */
import FeaturePage from '../layouts/FeaturePage.astro';
import heroImage from '../assets/hero_performance.webp';
import { enTranslations as t, localePath } from '../i18n';
import { solutionsUrl, SOLUTIONS_LIVE } from '../config';
import {
	CASCADE_EVIDENCE,
	CASCADE_DRIVERS,
	CONCURRENCY_EVIDENCE,
	RETRIEVAL_EVIDENCE,
	GOVERNANCE_EVIDENCE,
	INJECTION_CHART,
	OVERSIGHT_EVIDENCE,
	DELIBERATION_EVIDENCE,
	MEMORY_EVIDENCE,
	HARNESS,
} from '../data/benchmarks';

const title = 'Benchmarks & Evidence';
const description =
	'What the published research supports about LLM cascading cost, prompt-injection defences, human oversight, multi-agent deliberation, memory, JVM virtual threads and retrieval quality, what EDDI claims, and which numbers we have not measured yet.';

const enterpriseCtaHref = SOLUTIONS_LIVE ? solutionsUrl('en') : localePath('en', '/contact/');

const faqItems = [
	{
		question: 'Why does this page not contain EDDI benchmark numbers?',
		answer:
			'Because we have not run the harness yet, and publishing numbers we have not measured is how benchmark pages lose their credibility. What this page does publish is the evidence that the underlying mechanisms work, the conditions that determine how much they help, and the exact measurement plan, so that when the numbers land you can check them against a methodology that was fixed in advance.',
	},
	{
		question: 'How should EDDI\'s cascade cost figure be read?',
		answer:
			'EDDI quotes the range measured in production, 31% to 58% lower inference cost at held quality, rather than the benchmark ceiling. Published savings span roughly 30% to 98% depending on how many queries are genuinely easy, how well calibrated the escalation signal is, and how far apart the model tiers are priced. Your number depends on your workload, and nobody can quote it to you in advance.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Are virtual threads always faster?',
		answer:
			'No. The literature is consistent that virtual threads improve throughput on blocking, I/O-bound workloads and impose overhead on CPU-bound ones. One study found them slightly slower than platform threads on a CPU-bound parallel task, and a Quarkus-specific study found virtual threads did not match Quarkus-reactive in a resource-constrained container. EDDI\'s workload is I/O-bound, which is the case the mechanism suits, but that is an argument about workload fit rather than a universal claim.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Can we run these benchmarks ourselves?',
		answer:
			'That is the intent. EDDI is Apache 2.0 and self-hosted, so nothing prevents you measuring it on your own hardware with your own workload. Given that cost savings depend heavily on workload composition, your own measurement is worth more than ours.',
	},
];

const doiUrl = (doi: string) => `https://doi.org/${doi}`;
---

<FeaturePage
	title={title}
	description={description}
	heroImage={heroImage}
	lang="en"
	faqItems={faqItems}
	localized={false}
>

<aside class="verdict" aria-label="Summary">
	<p class="verdict-label">In short</p>
	<p class="verdict-body">
		The mechanisms EDDI relies on are well supported in the published literature.
		The <em>magnitude</em> of the benefit is workload-dependent in every case, and
		anyone quoting you a single number without asking about your workload is
		guessing. EDDI's own measurements are not published yet. This page says what
		the evidence supports, what it does not, and exactly what we will measure.
	</p>
</aside>

<h2>Why this page is shaped like this</h2>

<p>
	Vendor benchmark pages have a credibility problem, and it is deserved. The numbers
	are produced by the party they flatter, on hardware nobody names, against a
	baseline chosen after the fact.
</p>

<p>
	So this page separates three things that usually get blended together:
	<strong>what independent published research establishes</strong> about the
	mechanisms EDDI uses, <strong>what EDDI claims</strong> and how those claims
	should be read against that evidence, and <strong>what has not been measured</strong>,
	stated plainly rather than omitted.
</p>

<p>
	Every citation below resolves to a DOI, and each is labelled
	<strong>peer-reviewed</strong> or <strong>preprint</strong>. Several of the most
	widely quoted cascade results, including FrugalGPT, are arXiv preprints. That does
	not make them wrong, but you should weigh them accordingly, and we would rather
	label them than have you discover it. Several of these results also cut against
	EDDI, and they are included for that reason.
</p>

<h2 id="cascade">Model cascading: what the evidence supports</h2>

<p>
	Model cascading sends easy queries to a small model and escalates hard ones. The
	mechanism is well established and the savings are real. The <em>range</em> is very
	wide, and where you land in it is a property of your workload, not of the platform.
</p>

<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>Source</th><th>Reported result</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		{CASCADE_EVIDENCE.map((row) => (
			<tr>
				<td><a href={doiUrl(row.doi)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{row.study}</a></td>
				<td class="setting">
					{row.venue}<br />
					<span class:list={['src-tag', row.reviewed ? 'src-reviewed' : 'src-preprint']}>
						{row.reviewed ? 'peer-reviewed' : 'preprint'}
					</span>
				</td>
				<td>{row.result}</td>
				<td class="setting">{row.setting}</td>
			</tr>
		))}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="callout callout-warn">
	<p class="callout-label">Read the methodology, not just the headline</p>
	<p>
		The UCCI authors make a point worth carrying into any cascade claim you evaluate,
		including ours: their results use end-to-end routing on actual model outputs and
		measured GPU latency, <strong>not simulated routing from global accuracies or
		nominal API prices</strong>. A large share of published cascade savings come from
		simulation. Simulated savings are systematically more optimistic than measured ones,
		which is part of why the most carefully measured figure in the table above is also
		the lowest.
	</p>
</div>

<h3>What actually determines your number</h3>

{CASCADE_DRIVERS.map((driver) => (
	<div class="driver">
		<h4>{driver.title}</h4>
		<p>{driver.body}</p>
	</div>
))}

<h3>How to read EDDI's own figure</h3>

<p>
	EDDI's feature pages quote the two production measurements in the table:
	<strong>31% to 58% lower inference cost at held quality</strong>. We chose them over
	the benchmark results on purpose. They are the lowest numbers here, and the ones
	measured on real traffic rather than simulated, so they are the fairer expectation
	for a deployment you have not measured yet.
</p>

<p>
	The honest version is a conditional: if a large share of your traffic is routine, if
	your escalation signal is calibrated, and if there is a wide price gap between your
	tiers, savings in that range or above are achievable and the literature supports it. If any of
	those three is absent, expect substantially less. We would rather you arrive at your
	own number than adopt ours.
</p>

<h2>JVM concurrency: what the evidence supports</h2>

<p>
	EDDI runs on the JVM and uses virtual threads, on the argument that agent workloads
	are I/O-bound: they spend most of their time waiting on LLM responses, vector store
	queries, and tool calls. The literature supports that argument <em>for that workload
	shape</em>, and is equally clear about where it stops holding.
</p>

<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>Source</th><th>Reported result</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		{CONCURRENCY_EVIDENCE.map((row) => (
			<tr>
				<td><a href={doiUrl(row.doi)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{row.study}</a></td>
				<td class="setting">
					{row.venue}<br />
					<span class:list={['src-tag', row.reviewed ? 'src-reviewed' : 'src-preprint']}>
						{row.reviewed ? 'peer-reviewed' : 'preprint'}
					</span>
				</td>
				<td>{row.result}</td>
				<td class="setting">{row.setting}</td>
			</tr>
		))}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="callout callout-warn">
	<p class="callout-label">The inconvenient one</p>
	<p>
		A study of virtual thread integration in <strong>Quarkus</strong>, the framework
		EDDI is built on, found that virtual threads did not perform as well as
		Quarkus-reactive in a resource-constrained container, attributing it to a mismatch
		between Netty's assumptions and virtual threads. That is a directly relevant
		negative result and it belongs on this page. It bounds the claim: virtual threads
		buy a simpler programming model at competitive throughput, not a guaranteed win
		over every alternative concurrency design.
	</p>
</div>

<p>
	Two further qualifications the evidence supports. Virtual threads <strong>impose
	overhead on CPU-bound work</strong>, so "millions of threads" is a statement about
	I/O-bound capacity and not a general throughput claim. And as concurrency rises,
	<strong>memory rather than CPU becomes the binding constraint</strong>, which means
	heap configuration is part of any honest capacity number.
</p>

<h2>Retrieval quality: chunking dominates</h2>

<p>
	EDDI supports 8 embedding providers and 6 vector stores. The evidence says that
	choice matters far less than how documents are split, which is why our
	<a href={'/guides/build-a-rag-agent-with-chroma/'}>RAG guide</a> tells you to vary
	chunking before swapping models.
</p>

<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>Source</th><th>Reported result</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		{RETRIEVAL_EVIDENCE.map((row) => (
			<tr>
				<td><a href={doiUrl(row.doi)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{row.study}</a></td>
				<td class="setting">
					{row.venue}<br />
					<span class:list={['src-tag', row.reviewed ? 'src-reviewed' : 'src-preprint']}>
						{row.reviewed ? 'peer-reviewed' : 'preprint'}
					</span>
				</td>
				<td>{row.result}</td>
				<td class="setting">{row.setting}</td>
			</tr>
		))}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>
	Note the counterweight in that table: cluster-based semantic chunking did
	<em>not</em> beat simpler strategies on academic theses. More sophisticated chunking
	is not automatically better, which is another reason to measure on your own corpus
	rather than adopt a recommended default.
</p>

<h2 id="governance">Governance: where the rules are enforced</h2>

<p>
	EDDI's security model puts its rules in the engine, not in the prompt: tool
	approvals, allow-lists, vault references and the SSRF-safe HTTP client apply at the
	boundary where a tool call leaves the model. The research on prompt injection is the
	reason. Instructions hidden in a ticket, a web page or a tool result reach the model
	as ordinary text, and a model cannot reliably tell them apart from yours.
</p>

<figure class="chart" aria-labelledby="inj-cap">
	<svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-label="Share of injected attacks that led to an unsafe tool action, by defence: none 82.5%, safety prompt 25.6%, self-reminder 21.7%, spotlighting 18.3%, second LLM as judge 10%, static allowlist 5%, policy gate at the tool call 0%">
		{INJECTION_CHART.map((row, i) => {
			const y = 14 + i * 40;
			const w = (row.value / 100) * 380;
			const last = i === INJECTION_CHART.length - 1;
			return (
				<g>
					<text x="210" y={y + 17} text-anchor="end" class="chart-label">{row.label}</text>
					{w > 0
						? <rect x="220" y={y + 4} width={w} height="20" rx="4" class={last ? 'bar-accent' : 'bar'} />
						: <rect x="220" y={y + 4} width="3" height="20" class="bar-accent" />}
					<text x={220 + Math.max(w, 3) + 8} y={y + 18} class="chart-value">{row.value === 0 ? '0 of 240' : `${row.value.toFixed(1)}%`}</text>
				</g>
			);
		})}
	</svg>
	<figcaption id="inj-cap">
		Unsafe tool actions under injected instructions, by defence (NetInjectBench, 240 attacks,
		preprint). *The static allowlist also blocked every approved change, so it was unusable.
	</figcaption>
</figure>

<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>Source</th><th>Reported result</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		{GOVERNANCE_EVIDENCE.map((row) => (
			<tr>
				<td><a href={doiUrl(row.doi)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{row.study}</a></td>
				<td class="setting">
					{row.venue}<br />
					<span class:list={['src-tag', row.reviewed ? 'src-reviewed' : 'src-preprint']}>
						{row.reviewed ? 'peer-reviewed' : 'preprint'}
					</span>
				</td>
				<td>{row.result}</td>
				<td class="setting">{row.setting}</td>
			</tr>
		))}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="callout">
	<p class="callout-label">How this maps to EDDI, and what it does not prove</p>
	<p>
		EDDI's per-tool approval gates, glob allow and exempt lists, and vault-only
		credentials are execution-time boundaries of the kind these studies favour, and an
		approval binds to the resolved request rather than to a tool name. We have
		<strong>not</strong> measured EDDI against these benchmarks, and a gate is only as
		good as the policy behind it: an allow-list that is too wide lets the attack
		through, and one that is too narrow blocks the work, as the static allowlist above did.
	</p>
</div>

<h2 id="oversight">Human oversight: few gates, well placed</h2>

<p>
	Human approval gates are one of EDDI's core controls. The literature says they work,
	and also that human attention is a finite resource. A gate that fires on everything
	trains people to click Approve.
</p>

<figure class="chart" aria-labelledby="hitl-cap">
	<svg viewBox="0 0 640 110" role="img" aria-label="Critical failure rate: 72% for unconstrained agents, 16% with deterministic computation and human decision gates">
		<text x="210" y="31" text-anchor="end" class="chart-label">Unconstrained agents</text>
		<rect x="220" y="18" width={0.72 * 380} height="20" rx="4" class="bar" />
		<text x={220 + 0.72 * 380 + 8} y="32" class="chart-value">72%</text>
		<text x="210" y="71" text-anchor="end" class="chart-label">With human gates</text>
		<rect x="220" y="58" width={0.16 * 380} height="20" rx="4" class="bar-accent" />
		<text x={220 + 0.16 * 380 + 8} y="72" class="chart-value">16%</text>
	</svg>
	<figcaption id="hitl-cap">Share of AI-assisted research runs with a critical failure (280 runs, preprint).</figcaption>
</figure>

<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>Source</th><th>Reported result</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		{OVERSIGHT_EVIDENCE.map((row) => (
			<tr>
				<td><a href={doiUrl(row.doi)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{row.study}</a></td>
				<td class="setting">
					{row.venue}<br />
					<span class:list={['src-tag', row.reviewed ? 'src-reviewed' : 'src-preprint']}>
						{row.reviewed ? 'peer-reviewed' : 'preprint'}
					</span>
				</td>
				<td>{row.result}</td>
				<td class="setting">{row.setting}</td>
			</tr>
		))}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>
	That is why EDDI sets approval friction per endpoint and per tool, lets a gate time out
	to approve, reject, abort or wait, and detects a loop of identical pauses instead of
	asking the same person the same question again.
</p>

<h2 id="deliberation">Multi-agent deliberation: diversity and the vote</h2>

<p>
	EDDI's group conversations let several agents discuss, vote and decide. The research is
	more mixed than the enthusiasm around multi-agent debate suggests, and it shapes how
	the feature is built.
</p>

<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>Source</th><th>Reported result</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		{DELIBERATION_EVIDENCE.map((row) => (
			<tr>
				<td><a href={doiUrl(row.doi)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{row.study}</a></td>
				<td class="setting">
					{row.venue}<br />
					<span class:list={['src-tag', row.reviewed ? 'src-reviewed' : 'src-preprint']}>
						{row.reviewed ? 'peer-reviewed' : 'preprint'}
					</span>
				</td>
				<td>{row.result}</td>
				<td class="setting">{row.setting}</td>
			</tr>
		))}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>
	Three design choices follow. Members can run on <strong>different models</strong>, since
	diversity is what consistently helps. Decisions go to a <strong>formal vote</strong> with
	the tally and the losing side's dissent recorded, since voting carries most of the gain.
	And ballots are cast <strong>independently of each other</strong>, so one confident,
	persuasive member cannot talk the room into its answer during the vote itself.
</p>

<h2 id="memory">Conversation memory</h2>

<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>Source</th><th>Reported result</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		{MEMORY_EVIDENCE.map((row) => (
			<tr>
				<td><a href={doiUrl(row.doi)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{row.study}</a></td>
				<td class="setting">
					{row.venue}<br />
					<span class:list={['src-tag', row.reviewed ? 'src-reviewed' : 'src-preprint']}>
						{row.reviewed ? 'peer-reviewed' : 'preprint'}
					</span>
				</td>
				<td>{row.result}</td>
				<td class="setting">{row.setting}</td>
			</tr>
		))}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>
	EDDI keeps the full conversation in storage and controls what the model sees: long-term
	properties that outlive the context window, token-aware windowing, rolling summaries with
	a recall tool to drill back in, and Dream consolidation of persistent memory in the
	background. We have not benchmarked this against LongMemEval or LOCOMO.
</p>

<h2>What we have not measured</h2>

<div class="callout callout-gap">
	<p class="callout-label">Not yet published</p>
	<p>
		EDDI has no published first-party benchmark numbers. Rather than fill this section
		with figures produced under conditions chosen to flatter them, here is the harness
		we intend to run and publish, fixed in advance so the methodology cannot be tuned
		to the result.
	</p>
</div>

<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Metric</th><th>Why this metric</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		{HARNESS.map((item) => (
			<tr>
				<td><strong>{item.dimension}</strong></td>
				<td>{item.metric}</td>
				<td class="setting">{item.why}</td>
			</tr>
		))}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>Alongside the numbers, every run will publish the things that make a benchmark checkable:</p>

<ul>
	<li>Exact EDDI version, JVM version, and JVM flags.</li>
	<li>Hardware, including instance type, core count, and memory.</li>
	<li>Database engine and version, and whether it ran in or out of the cluster.</li>
	<li>The workload generator and the query distribution, published so it can be replayed.</li>
	<li>The baseline configuration, and why it is a fair comparison rather than a strawman.</li>
	<li>Raw results, not only summary statistics.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Measure it yourself</h2>

<p>
	The strongest argument for self-hosted, Apache 2.0 software in a benchmarks context
	is that you do not have to take our word for it. EDDI runs wherever Docker runs, so
	you can measure it on your hardware, with your workload, at your quality bar.
</p>

<p>
	Given how strongly cascade savings depend on workload composition, a measurement you
	run is worth considerably more than one we publish. The
	<a href={'/guides/self-host-eddi-with-docker-compose/'}>Docker Compose guide</a> gets
	you to a working deployment in about fifteen minutes.
</p>

<h2>Frequently asked</h2>

{faqItems.map((item) => (
	<div class="faq-item">
		<h3>{item.question}</h3>
		<p>{item.answer}</p>
	</div>
))}

